misc: decd: program the source route from the submitted descriptor

Moves the DECD source route out of shell and into the driver: seven geometry
words, source enable, two-plane format byte and the config commit, all derived
from the submitted descriptor.  Hardware-verified 2026-09-08 -- the colour-bar
test frame renders correctly with the driver programming the block and no shell
writes to it at all.

  decd 5600000.dec: DECD route: 1280x720 stride 1280 hw-format 3

THE COMMIT AT 0x05600014 RETIRES ON VSYNC.  This is the whole reason the patch
was hard, and it is not written down anywhere else.

Every shell recipe that ever worked wrote the registers through separate devmem
processes and then slept 100 ms -- six vsyncs -- before touching anything else.
Issued back to back from the kernel the commit gets microseconds, does not
retire, and the hardware silently ignores the configuration.  The registers
still read back exactly right, so the failure is invisible to a register dump:

  - the frame renders DOUBLED side by side at half height, the signature of a
    2-bytes-per-pixel fetch (each display row consumes two source rows, so even
    rows land left, odd rows right, and 720 rows end at display row 360)
  - a full dump of the afbd block (0x05600000..0x1FF), the top block
    (0x05700000..0xFF) and the whole composition page (0x05000000..0x87c) is
    BYTE-IDENTICAL between a working run and a broken one

The only differing words were +0x630..+0x648, which the 2026-08-31 composition
capture already identifies as live per-frame telemetry -- a symptom of the
different picture, not a cause.

So the route polls the latch until it reads 0 (consume-on-write) with a 50 ms
budget, three vsyncs of margin.  An earlier attempt polled 10 ms and gave up
before the first frame boundary ever arrived, which logged "source commit did
not retire" and reproduced the doubling exactly.

ORDERING.  The route is staged in frame_item_create() and applied from the
submit ioctl AFTER dec_reg_enable(), not before.  docs/ge2d-plane-open-re.md
records that the vendor never writes the format selector alone -- it is always
accompanied by dec_reg_mux_select(), dec_reg_top_enable() and dec_reg_enable()
-- and the recorded rule is likewise "after dec_reg_enable()".

Within the route, commit the config while the format byte still reads 0, then
switch the byte.  0x05600014 commits the SOURCE CONFIG; 0x0560006c publishes
the PLANE ADDRESSES.  Both are needed and they are not interchangeable.

The route is applied per stream, and re-applied whenever the source enable bits
are clear -- caching on geometry alone silently skips it after anything
disables the source between streams, such as the ordering rule that requires
the source be off across an IOMMU bypass flip.

item->format_shadow was hardcoded to 0 (= RGB888), which is why every trace
reported format 0 while the panel was fed NV12: the byte was only ever traced,
never programmed, so whatever U-Boot left in 0x05600011 stayed.  It now comes
from fmt_attr_tbl column 8.  Only the descriptor-format-6 -> hardware-format-3
path (linear 8-bit NV12) is hardware-verified; auto_route=0 falls back to
manual routing.

DELIBERATELY NOT INCLUDED: chroma gain 0x05140508 and plane selector
0x051c006c.  They live in the display engine, are not mapped by this driver,
and are shared with the MIPS logo path.  Routing the panel to video is a
display-ownership decision, not a decoder one.

Still undecoded: 0x05600024 = 0x002C004F, the crop origin, is carried through
as a constant from the verified recipe.

--- a/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_hw.c
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
 #include <linux/bitops.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/ktime.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
@@ -291,6 +292,94 @@
 	writeb((v & ~BIT(4)) | (on ? BIT(4) : 0), regs->workaround + 0x04);
 }
 
+/*
+ * dec_reg_route_nv12 -- program the source configuration for a linear two-plane
+ * YUV frame, in DECD's own register block.
+ *
+ * Hardware-verified 2026-09-08 (docs/reference/nv12-scanout-solved-2026-09-08).
+ * Until now this was applied by hand from shell before every run.
+ *
+ * TWO LATCHES, DIFFERENT JOBS -- this is the whole trick:
+ *
+ *   0x14  commits the SOURCE CONFIG    (geometry words + source enable)
+ *   0x6c  publishes the PLANE ADDRESSES (two-plane YUV path)
+ *
+ * Both are required.  Writing only 0x6c leaves the geometry and enable
+ * uncommitted: they read back correctly and the fetcher still has no valid
+ * picture, so it fetches nothing and the panel shows SOLID GREEN (Y=U=V=0
+ * through BT.601 clamps R and B to 0 and gives G ~= 135).  That cost four
+ * operator looks before it was found.
+ *
+ * ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: commit the config while the format byte still reads 0,
+ * then switch the byte to the two-plane format.  The plane addresses and the
+ * 0x6c publish are NOT done here -- the per-frame path already writes the four
+ * ring slots and calls dec_reg_set_dirty() after slot 3.
+ *
+ * The chroma gain (0x05140508) and the plane selector (0x051c006c) are
+ * deliberately NOT touched: they live in the display engine, are not mapped by
+ * this driver, and are shared with the MIPS logo path.  Routing the panel to
+ * video is a display-ownership decision, not a decoder one.
+ */
+void dec_reg_route_nv12(struct dec_reg_block *regs, u32 w, u32 h, u32 stride,
+			u8 hw_format)
+{
+	void __iomem *b;
+	int i;
+
+	if (!regs || !regs->afbd || !w || !h || !stride)
+		return;
+
+	b = regs->afbd;
+
+	writel(((h - 1) << 16) | (w - 1), b + 0x20);	/* crop, minus one */
+	writel(0x002C004F,                b + 0x24);	/* origin; constant from
+							 * the verified recipe,
+							 * not yet decoded */
+	writel((h << 16) | w,             b + 0x30);	/* picture size */
+	writel((h << 16) | w,             b + 0x48);
+	writel(((h / 2) << 16) | w,       b + 0x4c);	/* chroma is half height */
+	writel(stride,                    b + 0x40);	/* luma pitch */
+	writel(stride,                    b + 0x44);	/* chroma pitch */
+
+	/*
+	 * Commit the configuration, WAIT FOR THE LATCH TO CONSUME, then select
+	 * the two-plane format.
+	 *
+	 * The wait is the point.  The shell recipe this replaces wrote every
+	 * register through a separate devmem process, so the commit had ~10 ms to
+	 * retire before anything else touched the block.  Issued back to back from
+	 * the kernel it gets microseconds, and the result is a frame rendered
+	 * doubled side by side at half height -- a 2-bytes-per-pixel fetch -- with
+	 * a register dump that is byte-identical to a working run across the afbd
+	 * block, the top block and the whole composition page.  Only the timing
+	 * differs, and no dump can show it.
+	 *
+	 * 0x14 is consume-on-write: reading 0 means it retired.
+	 */
+	writel(0x03000013, b + 0x10);
+	writel(1,          b + 0x14);
+
+	/*
+	 * Must outlast a vsync: the commit retires on the frame boundary, which
+	 * is 16.7 ms at 60 Hz.  The first version polled 1000 x 10 us = 10 ms and
+	 * gave up before the first vsync ever arrived -- "source commit did not
+	 * retire" -- so the format byte was written while the latch was still
+	 * pending.  The shell recipe this replaces always had a 100 ms sleep here,
+	 * six vsyncs, which is why it never hit this.  5000 x 10 us = 50 ms is
+	 * three vsyncs of margin.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < 5000; i++) {
+		if (!readl(b + 0x14))
+			break;
+		udelay(10);
+	}
+	if (readl(b + 0x14))
+		pr_warn("sunxi-decd: source commit did not retire\n");
+
+	udelay(200);
+	writeb(hw_format,  b + 0x11);
+}
+
 /* IDA 0x39f8: *(result+8) = workaround, offset +12 */
 void dec_reg_set_dirty(struct dec_reg_block *regs, bool dirty)
 {
